  "feature1": {
    "prompt": "Enhance the contour() function so that when a boolean 2D array is passed as input without an explicit levels argument, the function automatically detects the boolean input and sets the default contour level to [0.5] (or [0, 0.5, 1] in the case of filled contours). Users often call plt.contour(boolean_2d_array, levels=[0.5], ...) to draw the boundary between True and False regions because the default behavior with non-boolean data produces many overlapping contours. The goal is to have the function recognize boolean arrays and render a single, meaningful contour that clearly separates the regions. This change should ensure that the contour plot accurately reflects the binary nature of the input data.",
    "hint_texts": "This part focuses on enhancing the functionality to handle boolean arrays. The implementation should add logic in the _process_contour_level_args function to check if the input data type is boolean (using np.issubdtype) and, if so, set the default levels appropriately. Similar modifications need to be applied in the tri/_tricontour.py module. Look at how default levels are determined and ensure that for non-filled contours the level is set to [.5] and for filled contours to [0, .5, 1].",
    "entrypoint": "Modify the file lib/matplotlib/contour.py in the _process_contour_level_args function by adding a second parameter (z_dtype) and a branch to detect boolean types. Also update the file lib/matplotlib/tri/_tricontour.py in the _contour_args function to extract the dtype from the z array and pass it to _process_contour_level_args.",
    "tests": {
      "FAIL_TO_PASS": "lib/matplotlib/tests/test_contour.py::test_bool_autolevel",
      "PASS_TO_PASS": ""
    }
  },
  "feature2": {
